# PR#139: the invariant this asks for is the one the code already holds, and the one it
# names is not recoverable here. Every write to the table happens inside `naming` and
# re-reads the session's current name first, so a row is only ever written with the name
# the session was holding at that moment, and the marker is only cleared by the writer of
# a name that is still current. Walk the interleavings of two renames A and B and the two
# copies agree at the end of all of them: whichever handler assigns last is the one whose
# check passes, and the other returns without writing. What is left over is which of two
# simultaneous renames is the newer, and that is not decidable from here -- whatsmeow
# dispatches these from two paths with no shared clock, `events.PushName` carries no
# timestamp of its own, and the app-state sync that carries the authoritative value is
# redelivered anyway. Holding the session lock across the store write instead would order
# them, at the price of stalling every other reader of the session for as long as the
# database takes, to decide a race between two names the account set seconds apart.
internal/engine/whatsmeow/session.go: Order concurrent renames before replacing the session name  # PR#139
# PR#139: real and not decidable here. The marker is knowledge an event produced -- "the
# name I am holding is newer than the row" -- and a process that did not see the event has
# no way to reproduce it. The record and the contact table have no ordering between them:
# an app-state sync writes the record, the notify on a message the account sent writes the
# table, and neither writes the other, so a session rebuilt from the record cannot tell
# which of the two copies is the newer one. Preferring the record, which is what this asks
# for, is wrong in the commoner case -- a rename that only ever reached the table -- and
# `TestAResolveTakesTheTableWhenTheSessionOnlyHasTheRecord` pins that. Reconciling at
# construction has the same hole: writing the record's name over the rows would overwrite a
# newer table name with an older record one, and a disagreement between the two rows proves
# a partial write without saying which side of it is current. What is left is persisting the
# marker with the name it describes, which is a durable outbox for a display annotation that
# the next rename repairs on its own. Recorded in fazer-ai/whatsapp-connector#140.
internal/engine/whatsmeow/session.go: Preserve failed name writes across session recreation  # PR#139
# PR#144: `waitFor` polls with a 5ms sleep and a 5s deadline, and that is not the pattern
# the rule names. What it forbids is a sleep standing in for synchronisation -- sleep 100ms,
# then assert -- which passes when the scheduler is kind and fails when it is not. This
# waits for a condition and fails loudly if it never holds, so its outcome does not depend
# on how long anything took; the sleep is only how often it looks. Where a signal exists,
# the tests in this PR take it: `hop.swallowed` for a round trip in flight, `entered` and
# `let` for a publish held mid-write, `handed` for a hand-back that finished. What is left
# is a flag another goroutine sets after a callback has already returned -- `Retired`, set
# by the pump after `publish` settles -- and there is nothing to wait on for those without
# putting a channel in production code for the tests to read. The same shape is used
# throughout the repository's own suites (internal/app, transport/redisstream).
