# PR#149: a two-release rollout is what you need when new behaviour breaks old
# participants, and nothing here does. Every pairing during a rolling deploy is either
# fixed or exactly main's behaviour today: new reader with new writer is the fix; new
# reader with old writer finds no mark and acknowledges the wake, which is what main
# does; old reader ignores the mark and acknowledges the wake, which is also what main
# does. At no point is any instance worse off than before the change, so there is no
# window to stage. The mark is a key nothing else reads, with a TTL of one lease, and
# an old instance that wins a lease without clearing a mark is covered by the fence:
# readers compare mark against holder, and a mark naming somebody who no longer holds
# the lease reads as nobody handing anything back. The one case an old binary leaves
# behind -- winning a lease under an instance id a mark still names -- costs wakes left
# pending for at most one TTL, with nothing lost. Staging it over two releases would
# add a flag and a second deploy to buy a property the additive shape already has.
internal/cluster/lease.go: Stage the hand-back marker for mixed-version fleets  # PR#149
# PR#149: the group this is about is the one that only exists when every lease on the
# instance is already past its usable life, and there is no ordering that closes it.
# Leases are renewed in one pipelined batch under one reading of the clock, so an
# instance's leases age together; a session adopted between ticks is stamped when it is
# acquired, which makes it newer and never older; and one whose renewal fails on its own
# is stopped by the sweep. Measured from outside on a running fleet, reading every PTTL in
# one EVAL rather than one call per key: across forty samples of thirty-three leases and
# forty of twenty-seven, the spread between the newest and the oldest was 0 ms in thirty of
# them and 1 ms in the rest, never more, with the smallest TTL seen 25.0 s of 30.
# So `ending` is empty, which is every ordinary shutdown, or it is everything, which is an
# instance that stopped renewing for about a whole TTL: a frozen process, a long pause, a
# suspended machine.
#
# In that state the exposure this names is one margin long. Local freshness at zero means
# the key in Redis has at most the margin left before it expires on its own, and after
# that a wake adopts the account normally; the loss needs a wake inside that window. What
# closing it would cost is a round trip in front of every socket, which is the finding two
# rounds ago -- and an instance that has not reached Redis for a whole TTL is not one
# whose mark would land anyway. Marking before the stops and stopping before the marks
# cannot both be true, and this side is the one where the residue is bounded by the margin
# rather than by how long a Redis outage lasts.
internal/session/manager.go: Mark each expiring hand-back before another stop can block  # PR#149
# PR#149: this one needs both groups to exist at once, and the two things it needs pull
# against each other. A lease only reaches the group with no room by going about a whole
# TTL without a renewal, which means the instance was not reaching Redis or was not
# running at all; a lease is only in the other group if it was renewed or acquired
# recently, which means it was. For the harm named here the releases in front also have to
# block, so Redis has to be away again by then. Measured on a running fleet, reading every
# PTTL in one EVAL rather than one call per key, an instance's leases carry the same
# expiry to the millisecond: across forty samples of thirty-three leases and forty of
# twenty-seven, the spread was 0 ms in thirty of them and 1 ms in the rest, never more.
# They are renewed in one pipelined batch under one reading of the clock; a session
# adopted between ticks is stamped when it is acquired, so it is newer than the rest and
# never older, and one whose renewal fails on its own is stopped by the sweep rather than
# kept.
#
# What is left is a sequence -- frozen for a TTL, resumed, an account adopted, Redis away
# again, SIGTERM -- against a fix that would put the releases behind every socket, which
# is the finding four rounds ago, or bound them so tightly they stop landing, which is
# what the mark exists to avoid. Marking before the stops and stopping before the marks
# cannot both be true; this is the ninth round on that one trade, and the residue on this
# side is one margin of lease rather than the length of an outage.
internal/session/manager.go: Stop fresh sockets before blocking on stale releases  # PR#149
